WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.589
I'm back Michelle from Lab Muffin here
with more beauty and science. Today I'll

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:09.050
be answering some common questions on
how sunscreen and makeup work together.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.410
Do I need to wear sunscreen every day
even though I'll be inside most of the

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:19.470
time? It depends. What are you trying to
achieve? If you're after skin cancer

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.810
prevention or anti-aging, wearing a daily
sunscreen can help with both. In the

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.250
biggest study on this so far, one group
of people wore sunscreen daily while the

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.570
other group only wore sunscreen when
they wanted to. After four and a half

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:38.460
years the skin of daily sunscreen users
had no detectable increase in aging and

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:44.489
were 24% less likely to show increased
signs of aging. Invasive melanomas were

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:49.739
almost four times less common in daily
sunscreen users. These benefits aren't

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.600
limited to places with lots of sun
either. Another study estimated that the

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.239
melanoma rate in Norway in older women
could drop by 18% with the daily use of

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:05.100
an SPF 15+ sunscreen. If you're
using photosensitizing ingredients in

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.580
your skincare then you'll want to use a
sunscreen because they can make your

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.860
skin more susceptible to damage by UV
light. Alpha hydroxy acids make your skin

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.740
more photosensitive. Retinoids can as
well. Yes, skincare ingredients can make

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.930
you age faster if you're not careful! If
you don't have a susceptibility to skin

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.829
cancer and you don't care about keeping
your skin looking nice then you don't

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:32.880
need to wear sunscreen daily. But because
you're here watching this video, then

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.189
chances are you probably should. Do I
need a separate sunscreen if my makeup

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:40.600
has SPF?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.690
Yes you'll probably need a separate
sunscreen sunscreen. SPF is tested at two

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:51.550
milligrams per square centimeter.
SPF generally scales linearly with the

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:56.320
amount you apply, so applying half of
this amount will give you around half of

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.670
the labelled SPF. With most makeup
products you probably won't be applying

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.080
anywhere near two milligrams per square
centimeter. On the average face this

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:11.400
translates to about 1.2 grams. Here's
what 1.2 grams of powder looks like... and

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:17.560
this is 1.2 grams of foundation. As you can see, these amounts

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.610
are way more than most people would
normally use. So how much protection

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.860
would you get with regular use? A 10 gram
powder lasts me about nine months of

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:32.080
daily use so I'm using 40 milligrams at
best, which is about 30 times less than

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:39.400
the testing amount. This gives me an SPF
of 1.7 for an SPF 50 product and SPF of

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.370
0.5 for a product labelled SPF 15. I
personally use one pump of foundation

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:52.060
which is 0.2 grams. For an SPF
50 foundation that gives me SPF 8 but

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:59.079
for an SPF 15 foundation that gives me
SPF 2.5. The only non-sunscreen product

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:03.640
that most people would use enough of is
moisturizer. So you'll either need a

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.880
moisturizer or sunscreen with SPF if
your makeup quantities don't measure up.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.400
What happens when I layer multiple
products with SPF?

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.540
Theoretically if you're applying two
milligrams per square centimetre and you

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.530
can't fit much more than that on your
face, the highest protection you can get

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:28.390
is the SPF of your highest product, since
any extra stuff you apply will wipe off.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:32.830
But on average, people don't apply this
much. So in general,

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:36.730
SPF will stack if you're putting more
sunscreen ingredients on your face and

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:41.650
you aren't applying it perfectly. In fact,
a study found that applying two layers

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.880
of sunscreen was a good way of getting
more UV protection - 2.5 times

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.230
more. Another possibility is that
different sunscreen ingredients can

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.060
destabilize each other and break down,
which means they'll stop working.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:59.590
A particularly sensitive one is avobenzone. In finished formulations the

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:02.950
ingredients might be protected in
certain ways, but if you're mixing them

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:07.450
on your skin it's very difficult to tell
if this is happening. It's a good idea to

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.110
avoid layering sunscreens containing avobenzone with products containing

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:17.260
octinoxate, titanium dioxide or zinc
oxide. It's safest to reapply the same

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:23.350
sunscreen on top or sunscreens with
similar ingredients. Can I mix sunscreen

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:30.400
into my makeup? This is not a good idea.
The key to a sunscreen working well is

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.960
having it form an even film on the
surface of your skin. It's incredibly

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.450
unlikely that you'll be able to mix it
evenly enough to get even coverage.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:44.590
You're also changing how the sunscreen
spreads on your skin. This means you'll

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.000
end up with microscopic holes in your
coverage. Is chemical or physical

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.930
sunscreen better?
There are two broad categories of

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.580
sunscreen ingredients: organic, which is
commonly called chemical, and inorganic,

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:05.060
which is commonly called physical. Both
have advantages and disadvantages. I'll

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:09.410
be referring to them as organic and
inorganic, since there are a few newer

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:13.970
ingredients that are technically both
chemical and physical and it gets really

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:19.730
confusing. Inorganic sunscreens are zinc
oxide and titanium dioxide, organic

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:24.740
sunscreens are everything else. Organic
sunscreens can achieve higher UVA

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:30.140
protectio,n which is great for anti-aging
and fading hyperpigmentation. They also

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.640
tend to have nicer textures than
inorganic sunscreens. Inorganic

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.010
sunscreens often have a white tint that
can be annoying if you have darker skin.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:43.669
However older organic ingredients that
are still quite common can cause

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:48.800
allergies. In the US many of the newer
chemical sunscreens aren't available at

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.310
all. I personally prefer new organic
sunscreens.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:57.710
There are also sunscreens which combine
both types. Use whatever sunscreen works

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:03.020
best with your budget, skin type and skin
concerns. Ideally it should be higher

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:09.919
than SPF 15, broad-spectrum and
water-resistant. Do I need to apply

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:15.919
chemical and physical sunscreens
differently? Both chemical and physical

00:06:15.919 --> 00:06:21.020
sunscreens work pretty much the same way. They form a film that absorbs UV light

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:26.180
and turns it into heat. A few sunscreen
ingredients like inorganic zinc oxide

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:31.790
and titanium dioxide and the newer
organic sunscreens Tinosorbs M &amp; S also

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:35.890
reflect and scatter a small proportion
of the incoming UV light.

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:41.050
All of these sunscreen ingredients work
without needing to react with the skin.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:45.500
This includes chemical sunscreens which
already work when they're in the tube or

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:51.140
applied to paper. But they do need to
form a continuous even layer between the

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:56.840
Sun and your skin to work well, so you
can treat them exactly the same way. This

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:04.270
is related to the next question...
What order should I apply my products in?

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:09.919
Before we start, a disclaimer. Sunscreen
is one of the best researched skincare

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:13.580
products, but there's very little
information on how sunscreen works with

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:18.039
other skincare products because it isn't
a priority in terms of money or health.

00:07:18.039 --> 00:07:23.479
But if we keep in mind how sunscreens
work then we can make a few educated

00:07:23.479 --> 00:07:28.340
guesses. Sunscreen needs to form an even
film on the skin and stay there to work

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:34.010
well. They're designed to spread and
stick to bare skin. Because of this you

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:37.190
should limit using products under your
sunscreen that are designed to stay on

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:40.250
the skin's surface. This includes makeup products like

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:45.530
powders, primers and foundation. Applying
moisturizer on top of your sunscreen can

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:51.199
decrease the protection you get. In one
study, applying moisturizer before sunscreen

00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:57.440
didn't affect SPF but applying moisturizer
after sunscreen reduced SPF by about

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:03.110
one-sixth. Water-resistant sunscreens
usually form occlusive films that block

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:08.240
out water, so any skincare you put on
top is going to be less effective at

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.139
getting into your skin.
Lots of sunscreens contain silicones

00:08:12.139 --> 00:08:16.250
which can help smooth out your skin
surface much like a primer, so they can

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:21.530
work well under makeup. So the most
practical order is skincare, sunscreen,

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:26.570
then makeup. This means your skincare can actually get to your skin and work, your

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:32.539
sunscreen is more likely to stay put, and
your makeup doesn't get messed up. How

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:37.669
should I apply my products so I don't
mess up my sunscreen? A very common

00:08:37.669 --> 00:08:41.959
question is, how do I apply my other
products so I don't mess up my sunscreen

00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:46.339
and make it ineffective?
Again the key thing to remember is that

00:08:46.339 --> 00:08:50.920
sunscreen needs to stay in a continuous
even layer to work most effectively.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:55.220
Sunscreens are generally designed to do
this automatically when applied to clean

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:59.810
skin and allowed to dry down. So what we
want to focus on is letting the

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:05.360
sunscreen form this film, then disturbing
it as little as possible. You don't want

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.959
to use anything under the sunscreen
that's designed to stay on top of the

00:09:08.959 --> 00:09:13.089
skin since it can interfere with how
well the sunscreen film forms and how

00:09:13.089 --> 00:09:18.129
it lasts. Both of the studies I dug up
found that moisturizers under sunscreen

00:09:18.129 --> 00:09:23.199
didn't affect SPF. But one of them found
that water resistance decreased. The

00:09:23.199 --> 00:09:26.889
authors suggest that it's because the
skin can't absorb as much of the sunscreen

00:09:26.889 --> 00:09:30.600
into the dead upper layers so the
sunscreen slides around on the surface.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:35.589
The oilier moisturizer had a larger
effect. So it's a good idea to avoid

00:09:35.589 --> 00:09:39.249
using products under sunscreen that are
designed to stay on the skin's surface

00:09:39.249 --> 00:09:44.709
like powders, primers and oily skin care
products. For products on top of

00:09:44.709 --> 00:09:49.180
sunscreen it's a bit worse. Sunscreen
dissolves in oil and most makeup

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:53.110
products like liquid foundations contain
oils, so they can act like makeup

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:57.129
removers when you apply them on top. How
badly this messes up your sunscreen

00:09:57.129 --> 00:10:02.620
seems to depend on a few things: the
formula of the sunscreen, the formula of

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:09.009
the product you're applying on top, and
how you apply it. What we know: applying

00:10:09.009 --> 00:10:12.459
moisturizer on top of a non
water-resistant sunscreen reduced the

00:10:12.459 --> 00:10:17.259
SPF by around 1/6. The oilier
moisturizers caused a bigger drop and

00:10:17.259 --> 00:10:23.380
made the sunscreen slide around more.
Some sunscreens last better when rubbed

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:28.000
with sand than others. Sunscreens that
claimed to be rub resistant stayed on

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:33.880
better in one study. Waiting 8 minutes
for sunscreen to dry made it more resistant

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:38.010
to removal when a t-shirt was worn on
top of it. So based on this information,

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:43.480
my advice would be to use a sunscreen
with a higher SPF than you need. It

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.800
should be water and rub resistant if
possible. Wait 8 minutes for the

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.490
sunscreen to dry down before applying
your makeup so it's less likely to be

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:56.620
disturbed. Gently pat on the makeup
rather than smearing it around. You can

00:10:56.620 --> 00:11:01.240
try using sponges or cushions to apply
foundation. They also give a nice finish

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:08.230
to your makeup. How can I stop my
sunscreen from balling up? If you've

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:12.130
tried a few sunscreens before you'll
find that some of them might ball up on

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:16.240
your skin when you apply them. This is
both funny looking and bad for sun

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:19.779
protection since rolls of sunscreen
can't protect your skin when they're no

00:11:19.779 --> 00:11:25.029
longer on your skin. How can you reduce
the chances of this happening? Firstly

00:11:25.029 --> 00:11:30.190
balling up can happen when your skincare
interacts with the sunscreen. Using the

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:34.060
sunscreen on bare skin or waiting long
enough between layers can get rid of the

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:39.700
issue. Using a moisturizer with SPF might
mean that you don't need skincare

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:44.470
underneath at all. Balling up also
happens with more friction. Patting your

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:49.240
sunscreen on gently can help. It can
increase the SPF you get as well - one

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:54.880
study found that rubbing the sunscreen
in too hard reduced SPF by 1/4. If all of

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.660
this doesn't work then it's probably
time to try to find a new sunscreen

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:03.310
that's more compatible with your skin.
Since my skin is naturally quite oily a

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:07.150
lot of sunscreens ball up on me, but I've
still managed to find a couple that

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:13.940
don't ball up at all. How do I look less
shiny? How can I get rid of all this oil?

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:18.480
Sunscreens are often quite thick and
oily to help the sunscreen ingredients

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.310
spread out and form an even film. The
problem is sometimes this can leave your

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:28.890
skin looking quite oily. Here's how you
can mattify it. Blotting papers: blotting

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:33.029
will remove some of the sunscreen and
lower protection but if you wait for the

00:12:33.029 --> 00:12:37.040
sunscreen to dry down for around 8
minutes most of it should stay put.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:42.350
Powder on top: a translucent powder on
top can soak up oil and reduce shine.

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:49.200
Again, try to pat this on gently. Find a
new sunscreen: sunscreen technology is

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.830
constantly improving and one of the
areas where there's a lot of attention

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:56.880
is on making sunscreens more
cosmetically elegant, which means more

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:01.050
pleasant to use. If a sunscreen isn't
working for you, try to find an

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:05.580
alternative.
Do I need to reapply sunscreen? How do I

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.470
reapply my sunscreen with my makeup on?

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:14.070
Sunscreen works less effectively as time
goes on, which is why bottles of

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:19.440
sunscreen always recommend reapplying
regularly. Sunscreen can be rubbed off if

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.910
you brush against it or go into the
water. The sunscreen film itself breaks

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:27.839
up and clumps up and gets thinner over
time because it evaporates and it

00:13:27.839 --> 00:13:31.279
interacts with the oil and water that
your skin produces throughout the day.

00:13:31.279 --> 00:13:36.000
This is worse if you sweat heavily. Some
older organic sunscreens aren't

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:38.370
photostable, which means they break down after

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:43.110
absorbing too much UV. Newer sunscreens
and inorganic sunscreens are photostable

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:47.160
which means they don't have this
specific problem, but the other two

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:51.930
factors mean reapplying is a good idea.
If you're reapplying sunscreen on top of

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:55.920
makeup, you'll either mess up your makeup
or you won't be applying enough

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:00.529
sunscreen to ensure complete protection.
There isn't really a way around it.

00:14:00.529 --> 00:14:05.730
Here's what I do. If I know I'm going to
need to reapply, such as if I'm doing a

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:09.390
lot of outdoor activities, I keep my
makeup simple with tinted

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:13.680
moisturizer and cream blush, then reapply
both sunscreen and makeup every two

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:18.390
hours. You can also limit the damage to
your makeup by applying with a patting

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:22.829
motion using a cushion applicator.
Sometimes I don't even bother with

00:14:22.829 --> 00:14:27.150
makeup. I also try to seek shade and wear
sun protective clothing where

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:31.530
possible. If I know I'm only going
outside for a short period, like if I'm

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:35.700
going on a walk after work, then I pack a
light sunscreen that works well on top

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:41.520
of makeup such as a powder or spray with
SPF. I also try not to stay in the sun,

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.750
and wear a hat and sunglasses. Some
people have also managed to find a

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.740
sunscreen-makeup combo that works when
you apply with a cushion, but again you

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:54.150
probably won't get enough for full
protection. If I know I'm only going to

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:57.960
get incidental sun, like if I'm heading
home after work on the train in summer,

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:02.330
then I don't bother reapplying and just
try not to get the window seat.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:07.590
Keep in mind that the Nambour study found
all those benefits of sunscreen with an

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:14.220
SPF 16 product that was around in 1992.
Sunscreen SPFs have gone up a lot since

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:18.690
then, the formulations are far more
sophisticated and sunscreen ingredients

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:22.500
have improved a lot as well. So even if
you aren't keeping your sunscreen

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:28.050
perfect, it's probably still making a big
difference. That's all from me today! Send

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:32.070
me all your questions, if you like this
video please subscribe, and check out my

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:50.499
blog for more beauty science :)

